solution:
Check each neighbouring pair on its own. A pair is a valid step when the words have the same length and differ in exactly one position.

```js
function isLadder(words) {
  for (let i = 1; i < words.length; i++) {
    const a = words[i - 1];
    const b = words[i];
    if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
    let changed = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < a.length; j++) {
      if (a[j] !== b[j]) changed++;
    }
    if (changed !== 1) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

The tests probe three easy mistakes. Comparing only up to the shorter word's length lets `plan → plain` through. Accepting "at most one" difference lets a repeated word (`cat → cat`) through. And checking whether the words share the same letters, rather than the same letters *in place*, accepts the swap `abcd → abdc`, which changes two positions.
